<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/20th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Revolution</p><h1 class="enc-title">Saur Revolution</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1978">1978</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Saur Revolution was the coup of 27–28 April 1978 in which army units loyal to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peoples-democratic-party-of-afghanistan">People's Democratic Party of Afghanistan</a> overthrew and killed President <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mohammad-daoud-khan">Mohammad Daoud Khan</a>. Named after the Afghan month in which it took place, it brought a communist government under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nur-muhammad-taraki">Nur Muhammad Taraki</a> to power. Its radical reforms and repression provoked a rural revolt that led to the Soviet intervention of 1979.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/saur-revolution.jpg" alt="Saur Revolution" width="320" height="215" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Cleric77 · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Day_after_Saur_revolution_in_Kabul_(773).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mohammad-daoud-khan">Mohammad Daoud Khan</a> took power in the 1973 coup that overthrew the monarchy of his first cousin, King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mohammad-zahir-shah">Mohammad Zahir Shah</a>, with the help of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peoples-democratic-party-of-afghanistan">People's Democratic Party of Afghanistan</a> (PDPA), and founded the first Republic of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/afghanistan">Afghanistan</a>. Coup attempts against him followed in 1973, 1974 and 1976. The PDPA was split between two factions, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/parcham">Parcham</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khalq">Khalq</a>. On 17 April 1978 a prominent Parchamite, Mir Akbar Khyber, was assassinated; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nur-muhammad-taraki">Nur Muhammad Taraki</a> blamed the government, and large protests broke out in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kabul">Kabul</a> on 19 April. Daoud ordered the protesters and the party leadership suppressed, and most PDPA leaders, including <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/babrak-karmal">Babrak Karmal</a>, were arrested.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hafizullah-amin">Hafizullah Amin</a>, under house arrest, instructed the party's Khalqist army officers to act. On 27 April tanks that had been placed around the Arg, the presidential palace, turned their guns on it, and the 4th and 15th Tank Brigades began firing. Fighter aircraft attacked the palace with rockets, and in the evening Radio Afghanistan announced that the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khalq">Khalq</a> were overthrowing the government. Fighting spread through <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kabul">Kabul</a>, and a column of tanks attacked the Air Force headquarters. In the night armoured units were sent to Jalalabad against loyalist officers of the 11th Division, with a death toll said to be near 1,000.</p><p>On 28 April soldiers entered the palace to arrest Daoud, who fired a pistol at them and was killed with his brother Naim. Most of his family, women and children included, were killed there too, as were the defence minister, the interior minister and the vice-president. The deaths of as many as 2,000 military personnel and civilians have been reported, though a former Afghan officer claimed only about 40. Soviet advisers had been told of the coup some hours before it began, but there is no convincing evidence that the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soviet-union">Soviet Union</a> planned it.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>A civilian government under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nur-muhammad-taraki">Nur Muhammad Taraki</a>, with Karmal and Amin in its cabinet, was formed, but the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khalq">Khalq</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/parcham">Parcham</a> alliance was brief: by July most Parchamites had been removed, and in September 1979 Amin overthrew and executed Taraki. From late October 1978 the party launched drastic reforms, including a new red flag, a ban on usury and land redistribution, and it declared the equality of the sexes. Its repression, which Barnett Rubin compared in scale to that of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abdur-rahman-khan">Abdur Rahman Khan</a>, included mass arrests, torture and executions; more than 27,000 are reported to have been killed in Pul-e-Charkhi prison alone.</p><p>The first anti-government revolts began in Kunar Province in October 1978, and an uprising at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herat">Herat</a> in 1979 left most provinces under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guerrilla-warfare">guerrilla</a> control. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soviet-union">Soviet Union</a> invaded in December 1979, and insurgents fought Soviet troops and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peoples-democratic-party-of-afghanistan">PDPA</a> government until the Soviet withdrawal in 1989.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Saur Revolution” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The People's Democratic Party of Afghanistan seized power in the <b>Saur Revolution</b> of April 1978, killing President Daoud Khan of the Republic of Afghanistan (1973-1978).</blockquote><a href="/state/democratic-republic-of-afghanistan">Democratic Republic of Afghanistan</a></li><li><blockquote>On 27 April 1978 army officers loyal to the People's Democratic Party killed Daoud and his family in the <b>Saur Revolution</b>, establishing the Democratic Republic of Afghanistan.</blockquote><a href="/state/republic-of-afghanistan-1973-1978">Republic of Afghanistan (1973-1978)</a></li></ul></section>
